## Negative Tests For Gates

Any feature that GATES, REFUSES, VALIDATES, AUTHORIZES, or ATTESTS must ship
with negative tests that fail when the gate admits what it is supposed to
reject. A positive test proving the happy path passes is not evidence that the
gate works; it is evidence that the gate is reachable.

This is the dominant defect class in this repository's 2026-07-28..08-03 waves.
Every instance below was found because a reviewer deliberately attacked the
gate, not because anyone read the code:

- the fact-assay attested a COLD child that never inherited the context, because
  it checked inventory, session id, digests, and output shape, but never
  inheritance;
- the assay fork had repository tool access, so it could answer from disk while
  the base was false;
- the builder attestation accepted a correctly-shaped forged digest that the
  caller minted itself;
- clearance enforcement existed but no production path called it, and counting
  tests around a fake proved nothing;
- an unknown assay event preserved a false zero-tool observation;
- a provider capability was declared absent to disable a flaky check, and the
  claim did not reproduce (9/11 success).

## Standard Negative Shapes

Use these names in checklists, verdicts, and test names. They are the shapes a
negative test must cover and the shapes a reviewer looks for first.

| Shape | What it looks like when nobody tests for it | What the negative test must do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Forged or self-minted evidence | The caller supplies the proof that the gate checks, and a correctly-shaped value passes. | Mint the evidence in the test the way a caller could, and require refusal. |
| Absent evidence treated as satisfied | Missing input takes the permissive branch; the suite only ever supplies the input. | Remove the evidence entirely and require refusal, not a default-pass. |
| Check present but uncalled from production | The guard is implemented and unit-tested by direct call; no production path invokes it. | Drive the real entry point — launch, materialize, resolve, publish — and name the production call site. |
| Bypass path around the check | One entry point enforces; a second, older, or retry/renewal path does not. | Exercise every entry point that reaches the protected state, including retry, renewal, resume, and recovery, and every delivery surface that carries the rule — a contract clause reaching one context profile and not the other is this shape. |
| Capability claim that does not reproduce | A capability is declared absent or present to make a flaky check go away. | Re-run the claim enough times to state a real rate, or report unknown. |

## Derive Completeness From An Independent Source

Completeness is derived from the pinned source or generated artifact, never
enumerated by hand. When a registry, schema set, or declared scope defines the
members, derive the expected set from that source and fail when any member lacks
enforcement. This rule comes from five consecutive review rounds that each
closed exactly the hand-picked members named by the previous reviewer; the next
unlisted member defeated every list.

A completeness test that reads the implementation's own table is circular and
can never detect a missing entry: removing the entry also removes it from the
test's expected set. Derive the expectation from the normative source of truth,
not from the code under test. This distinction was what let the next Story's
round-1 review demand a source-derived completeness test instead of accepting
the producer's two selected cases.

## Prove Exact Bounds At The Production Entry

Prove every bound in both directions at the production entry: accept at the
limit and refuse past the limit. Character bounds count characters rather than
bytes. The motivating failure was a value containing 3,999 characters but
5,999 bytes that production rejected with a byte comparison even though the
normative `string[1..4096]` bound permitted it; helper-only or ASCII-only tests
could not expose that narrowing.

## Disable Every Refusal Clause

Disable each refusal clause individually. If the suite stays green, that clause
is a hole unless the test or evidence documents it as provably subsumed and
names the check that subsumes it. This is measured rather than hypothetical: in
one file, disabling 110 clause sites produced 45 surviving mutants, and 12 of
those survivors were genuine widenings.

Numeric-bound mutation and clause-disablement are different classes, and one
harness rarely generates both. Numeric mutation finds off-by-one defects but
cannot express disabling a tagged-union arm, cross-field equality, or grammar
predicate; clause disablement finds those omissions but cannot move a numeric
boundary. The 110-site clause run exposed widenings that the numeric harness
could not generate, so run both classes.

## Check Refusals Against The Normative Source

A green test can defend a bug when it asserts a refusal that the normative
source does not require. One observed test required production to reject values
that the pinned specification permitted, so the suite protected the narrowing
instead of detecting it. Whenever a test encodes a refusal, check that refusal
against the pinned source rather than copying current behaviour into the
assertion.

## Eliminate Ambient Test Inputs

No test may depend on ambient environment. Fixtures set explicit identity and
all other values that could otherwise come from global configuration, the OS,
or developer-machine state. A developer machine let Git derive an author name
from the OS and passed the suite, while a CI runner with an empty GECOS field
failed with `empty ident name`; only CI can catch this class, so the fixture
must make its identity deterministic before CI exercises it.

## Prove A Bound By Narrowing, Not By Deleting

A test that removes a gate entirely proves only that the gate exists. It says
nothing about whether the gate covers the right class. Restrict the gate to a
narrower condition instead and require a named test to fail.

Two repository incidents where a narrowed gate survived a green suite:

- **BUG-260801-3kiloe F1** — the recovery successor cap was proven only by total
  removal. Narrowing it to provider-limit signals left every other recoverable
  failure unbounded, with the whole suite still green.
- **TASK-260802-17wetr F16** — thirteen cycles later, the same shape: the
  probe-lease cap survived deletion tests while the renewal path escaped it.
- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 1** — this contract itself. The reviewer clauses
  landed in the role body and the shipped test checked the role file and the
  lite template separately, so both halves were green while the composed lite
  prompt carried neither. Assert on the composed production artifact, not on the
  pieces it is built from.
- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 2** — the same contract, one level up, and the
  clearest statement of the difference between the two shapes. The cycle-1 fix
  did assert on the composed prompt, but it called the composition helper
  directly and handed it the role, task-class filter, and profile itself.
  Production builds those inputs inside the spawn entry point, so a mutant
  narrowing only the real lite-reviewer routing just before composition left the
  test green while an actual lite reviewer lost its gate-defeat licence. A test
  that supplies the inputs is testing the helper, not the path. Obtain the
  artifact the way the consumer does — here, read the prompt file recorded on
  the run manifest after driving the entry point — so role resolution, the
  task-class filter, and profile resolution are all under test rather than
  assumed.

Three traps when running the mutants themselves, all hit while proving this file:

- **The mutant must be re-run with `-count=1`.** `go test` caches on package
  sources; a test that reads `.roles/*.md` or any file outside its package at
  runtime returns a stale cached PASS after that file is mutated. A cached green
  is not a surviving mutant, it is an unexecuted test.
- **A passing assertion may be satisfied by the wrong source.** Two mutants of
  the producer DoD entries survived because the phrase asserted also appears,
  differently worded, in the shared spawn template. Choose assertion wording
  that only the intended source can supply, then narrow that source and require
  the named test to fail.
- **Restore the mutant from a copy, not from git.** `git checkout <file>`
  restores from the index, which silently discards uncommitted work in that file
  — during this task it reverted the whole cycle-2 change, not just the mutant.
  Copy the file aside before mutating, restore from the copy, and verify the
  restoration by diffing against it before rerunning the gates.

## A Gate Publishes Its Coverage As A Ratio

Prose about what a gate checks cannot distinguish 7 of 7 from 3 of 7. Every
statement such a description makes is usually true — it is true of what the gate
can see, and silent about what it cannot. The longer and more careful the
description, the more thorough the gate looks.

Report the fraction of intended targets the gate actually reaches, and assert
that fraction so it reddens when a target drops out of reach. A target no mutant
can redden is not covered, however elaborate the structure around it.

Three defects of this exact shape, each found only once a ratio existed:

- an AST call graph that recorded an edge only for a direct identifier call
  covered 3 of 7 guarded functions while every pin passed; the package's real
  dispatch was a function-value table, and the blind spot was invisible from
  inside;
- a classifier keyed on prose that a sibling gate was actively rewriting silently
  dropped 8 rows, taking a suite from 107 executed subtests to 66, while its
  completeness check `count == 0` reported nothing, because four is not zero;
- an ownership gate that proved a symbol exists and a case is linked reported 48
  sections as owned; measured against the specification, those bindings
  discharged 2 of 394 normative clauses.

A non-empty check on a derived set is the recurring form. `len(x) > 0` answers a
different question from `len(x) == expected`, and only the second notices a
narrowing.

## A Blind Spot Is Not An Empty Set

"Nothing is there" and "nothing this checker can see" are different facts, and
only the second is a statement about the checker. Reporting the first when you
mean the second converts a limitation into a clean bill of health, and the reader
has no way to tell.

An ownership gate scanned for uppercase RFC 2119 keywords. Sections stating their
obligations in declarative present tense scored zero, were bucketed as carrying
no obligations, and were admitted with no evidence required. Its documentation
said "there is no obligation to enumerate". The true sentence was "the section
carries no keyword line, so the scanner cannot see its obligations" — and 19 of
151 headings were free-admission surface the moment they were bound, including a
19-row exit-code registry and a closed manifest specification.

Two rules follow:

- State the bound of what the checker can see, next to what it reports. A
  residual class that is named can be attacked later; one that is implied cannot.
- Do not let the bucket for "nothing to check" also be the bucket that skips the
  check. Where absence of measurable obligation is a legitimate state, require an
  explicit reviewed justification for it, so the weakest entries are the ones
  that must say the most rather than the least.

## An Absence And A Failure To Read Are Different Facts

Absence is a legitimate state with a defined fallback. A failed, partial, or
malformed read is evidence of a problem. Treating the second as the first lets
the fallback launder a fault into a normal-looking value, and a fallback defined
for absence must never fire on a read failure.

Each of these was a separate defect in this repository:

- a non-empty value read as verified;
- an unknown scanner version read as clean;
- a partial provenance record read as absent;
- a zero pid start time read as alive;
- a malformed `api_error_status` read as missing;
- an unparsed envelope read as no envelope — this one produced a false provider
  suppression that would have hidden a healthy provider for hours.

## Prove, Or Report Nothing

Where a property cannot be established, report unparsed or unknown rather than
inferring it from a proxy signal. A plausible guess is worse than an explicit
unknown, because callers act on it.

Incident: headless top-level inference in the Claude classifier was unsound in
principle and produced six variants before it was deleted rather than
strengthened.

## Many Variants, One Unowned Decision

A long run of same-shaped variants usually means a decision has more than one
owner, not that there are many bugs. Eleven framing variants stopped reproducing
only when structure got a single scanner and a single state type; every per-site
patch before that produced the next variant. The operative wording is in
`.roles/orchestrator/role.md`: unify the decision, do not patch each site.

## Reviewer Licence

Reviewers are explicitly licensed to attempt to defeat a gate and to reject a
change whose only evidence is positive-path. Grep for callers before believing a
guard is in force. The operative wording is in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`.

## Exact-Evidence Reuse Is Not Full Replay

A green validation result is evidence about ONE candidate: the source and test
tree it ran against, the suite and configuration it ran with, and the
environment it ran in. Progressive checks may reuse that exact result while
all four identities are unchanged; the moment any of them differs, the result
is evidence about a different candidate and a rerun is required. The runtime
binding — which identities are hashed, what `reused_from_revision` records,
and why an absent or malformed environment identity forces a reasoned rerun —
is [docs/delivery-validation.md](../docs/delivery-validation.md). This page
states the shapes a plan and a reviewer check against.

- **Freeze the candidate before execution.** Evidence is bound to a captured
  tree, never to "the worktree as it is now". A result produced while the tree
  was still moving is evidence about no candidate at all.
- **Full replay needs a reason.** Re-running the whole configured suite is
  justified by exactly three things: a changed input identity, coverage the
  reused evidence does not carry, or a recorded diagnostic reason. "To be
  safe", "the runbook always does", or a retry that lost track of what already
  ran are not reasons, and each burns the same budget the incident measured.
- **Reuse never disarms the reviewer.** An independent reviewer retains
  targeted adversarial discretion over a reused green: the attack described in
  `Licence To Defeat A Gate` is against the candidate, not against the log,
  and a reused result is one more input to attack, not a waiver.
- **References and digests replace duplicated corpora.** A manifest of
  content digests proves what was checked; a second copy of the corpus proves
  only that the bytes were attached again. Portable archives are for a named
  consumer that cannot resolve references, and for nothing else.

## Refusal Rows: Research And Validation Budgets

The rows below are what the guidance REFUSES, and the two controls are what it
must keep admitting. Check a research plan, a rework brief, or a validation
decision against both halves: a rule that refuses everything satisfies the
first half and fails the second. Each refusal row names the observed incident
behind it (`TASK-260909-2ax2l1` retrospective and addendum).

| # | Scenario | Verdict | Why, and what the incident looked like |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| R1 | An agent justifies a second serial research prerequisite by consistency alone — "the sibling leaf has a gate, so this class needs one too", "the harness should also cover", symmetry with a prior leaf | **Refused.** A second serial prerequisite needs a checked spec gap (`solution-architect` rule 3: the missing piece, the requirement left incomplete, the spec sections checked) and its own budget; otherwise the finding is a focused regression in the owning leaf | Three nested gate leaves (contract gate, atom shapes, codepoints) were spawned from `repeat-of:` verdicts; production coverage stayed 0/28 vectors, 0/32 guards |
| R2 | Reuse of a green result after the harness, the source tree, the test tree, the configuration or the environment changed | **Refused.** Any changed identity makes the evidence about a different candidate; rerun with the diagnostic reason recorded. An earlier empty candidate never exempts a later source change | Reviewer anchors on a stale producer/revision are documented in two verdicts; a candidate was reviewed against a tree it no longer was |
| R3 | A gate claimed green with logs missing, truncated, unreadable, or ending before the last command | **Refused.** A failed or partial read is not a pass and not an absence; report which commands ran, which exit codes were observed, and mark the rest unrun. A cached `ok` on a package that reads files outside its module is this row | A 20-command suite timed out at command 13 of 20 after twelve greens; the seven unrun commands are unrun, not passed |
| R4 | A retry, resume, recovery or republish that starts its budgets from zero | **Refused.** Worker time, artifact bytes and serial depth accumulate across every continuation of the same leaf; a continuation inherits the budget position it was cancelled at | 13 of 42 runs were cancelled continuations; the chain re-entered full publication validation on an empty-delta research handoff until an operator directive stopped it |
| C1 | An unchanged green candidate: the same frozen tree, suite, configuration and environment, presented again for a later check | **Admissible.** Reuse the exact evidence; do not replay. The reviewer may still attack the candidate | The positive control for R2 and R3: identical identities, readable digest-matching log |
| C2 | A legitimate research-only Change Request with an empty `repository_delta`, whose task-scoped outcomes are attached and whose decision is recorded | **Admissible.** Publish it for independent review, skip source validation as `docs/delivery-validation.md` records, and route to the first implementation slice | The positive control for R1 and R4: one bounded contract task is the intended shape; twelve consecutive empty revisions with no consuming slice is what R1 and R4 refuse |


